{"gid":"2026_04_TEN_BAL","week":4,"away":"TEN","home":"BAL","away_col":"#4FC3E8","home_col":"#9E86FF","spread":8.5,"total":47.5,"fav":"BAL","hpts":28,"apts":20,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 4 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> BAL are run-first (30% pass when leading) into a TEN front that bleeds <strong>125 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.53 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>BAL</strong> host the <strong>TEN</strong> as favorites of <strong>8.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>47.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>BAL 28\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>39% of its snaps with BAL in front</strong>, <strong>35% within one score</strong>, and <strong>26% with BAL chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>BAL should control</strong>. A favorite of 8 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>BAL</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>TEN</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>TEN</strong> to run more total plays than BAL \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for TEN's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Tony Pollard</strong> \u2014 BAL are stingy against the run (94 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Cam Ward</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 BAL allow <strong>257 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 BAL concede <strong>74 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Rashod Bateman</strong> (WR2) \u2014 TEN leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>Tony Pollard</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 BAL give up 22 rec yds/game to backs, and TEN target the RB on 18% of throws.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is a bell-cow (76% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.53 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Zay Flowers</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 TEN surrender 0.53 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans BAL.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Derrick Henry</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile TEN throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong>.</p><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>BAL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>BAL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>30%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>BAL</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>15 carries for 75 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as BAL salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Zay Flowers</strong> (6 targets, 56 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when BAL lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mark Andrews</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>TEN \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>TEN are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 TEN project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tony Pollard</strong>'s rushing projection (14 carries, 62 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, TEN funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 53 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 23 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone